CML-to-Physical Home Lab — IKEv2/IPsec FlexVPN Implementation and Troubleshooting
Project: Hybrid Virtual and Physical Network Infrastructure
Technology: Cisco IOS, Cisco Modeling Labs (CML), VMware, IKEv2, IPsec, FlexVPN, NAT Traversal, Virtual Tunnel Interfaces, Static Routing
Implementation date: November 24, 2026
Purpose: Establish a routed, encrypted connection between a remote CML environment and a physical Cisco home lab, preparing the infrastructure for future BGP implementation.
1. Project overview
The objective of this project was to extend an existing physical Cisco networking lab into a hybrid networking environment by connecting Cisco Modeling Labs (CML), running remotely on a Windows computer through VMware, to physical Cisco networking equipment at home.
Before this implementation, the physical home lab already supported remote management through a certificate-based IKEv2/IPsec VPN. The Windows computer could establish a VPN connection to the physical Cisco router and remotely manage the networking devices.
However, allowing the Windows computer to access the physical network did not automatically allow virtual routers inside CML to communicate with the physical infrastructure.
The project therefore required solving several separate networking problems:
Overlapping IP address spaces.
VMware NAT and bridged networking behavior.
CML external connectivity and System Bridge configuration.
Windows IP forwarding.
Static routing and return-path reachability.
Remote-access VPN traffic selector limitations.
NAT Traversal through multiple network boundaries.
IKEv2 proposal and authentication configuration.
IPsec transform sets and profiles.
Dynamic Virtual Tunnel Interfaces (DVTI).
Static Virtual Tunnel Interfaces (SVTI).
IKEv2 route exchange and acceptance.
Separation of remote-access and router-to-router VPN services.
Tunnel verification and preparation for dynamic routing.
The resulting design established a dedicated router-to-router IPsec tunnel rather than relying on the Windows computer to forward CML traffic through its existing remote-access VPN.
