6. Problem 1 — Overlapping network subnets
6.1 Problem identification
The first significant problem was an overlap between the CML internal NAT subnet and the physical wireless network used by the Windows host.
The CML NAT network used a /24 subnet, while the Windows wireless interface was connected to a larger /22 subnet.
Although the network addresses appeared different when viewed individually, the entire CML /24 subnet was contained within the address range of the wireless /22 subnet.
For privacy, the actual addresses have been replaced with illustrative networks below.
The CML NAT subnet was entirely contained inside the physical wireless network's address range.
A /22 contains 1,024 IPv4 addresses and encompasses four consecutive /24 networks.
Consequently, the original addressing arrangement introduced ambiguity between virtual and physical network destinations.
6.2 Why this was a problem
The overlapping networks could cause traffic intended for one environment to be interpreted as belonging to another environment.
Potential consequences included:
Incorrect outgoing interface selection.
Unexpected ARP requests.
Incorrect next-hop selection.
Inconsistent NAT behavior.
Unreachable destinations.
Incorrect return paths.
Difficulty distinguishing virtualization problems from VPN problems.
Overlapping networks do not automatically break every NAT implementation because isolated routing domains can reuse addresses. However, they make direct routing between those environments problematic and complicate troubleshooting.
6.3 Windows troubleshooting commands
