12. Problem 3 — IPsec traffic selector limitation
12.1 Why the Windows forwarding design failed
The existing Windows remote-access VPN was established for an individual VPN client.
The negotiated IPsec security association covered the Windows VPN client's assigned address, represented as a /32 host address.
V1, however, generated packets using its own VMware underlay address.
Even if Windows successfully routed the packet toward its VPN interface, the original source address did not necessarily match the negotiated IPsec traffic selectors.
12.2 Routing versus encryption
A routing table determines where a packet should be forwarded.
An IPsec security association determines which traffic is eligible for protection under that security association.
These are separate mechanisms.
A valid static route does not automatically expand the IPsec traffic selectors.
Similarly, enabling Windows IP forwarding does not renegotiate the IPsec security association to include additional source networks.
