16.4 Authentication versus transport addressing
The IP address observed in the outer IP packet is not necessarily the same as the IKEv2 identity.
This is particularly important when NAT is present.
IKEv2 identity
    Identifies the VPN peer during authentication.

Outer source address
    Address visible after NAT translation.

Tunnel interface address
    Logical address used inside the encrypted tunnel.
A router behind VMware NAT may appear to the remote headend using a completely different outer source address and UDP source port.
This does not prevent IKEv2 authentication when the identity and keyring configuration are compatible.
17. Phase 10 — IKEv2 authorization policy
17.1 Purpose
IKEv2 authentication verifies the identity of the remote peer.
IKEv2 authorization determines what configuration and routing information can be exchanged or accepted after authentication.
The CML VPN required authorization behavior suitable for a routed tunnel rather than an individual remote-access client.
17.2 Authorization policy
The following commands were central to the final design:
crypto ikev2 authorization policy CML-AUTH
 route set interface
 route accept any

exit
17.3 route set interface
This command enables the exchange of interface-related routing information through IKEv2 authorization.
It allows the peer to learn the tunnel interface address through the FlexVPN configuration exchange.
This was important because R2 used a dynamically created, unnumbered Virtual-Access interface rather than a conventional statically addressed point-to-point interface.
17.4 route accept any
This command allows the router to accept routes supplied by the authenticated IKEv2 peer.
It resolved the requirement for the dynamically established tunnel to learn the remote tunnel address.
However, it is broader than an explicitly filtered route-acceptance policy.
The final design retained this setting because of the available IOS command options. Its security implications are discussed later in the report.
