24. Phase 17 — NAT Traversal
24.1 NAT boundaries
The dedicated IPsec connection still needed to traverse multiple network boundaries.
The remote side used VMware NAT and the physical Internet access network.
The home side used the residential AT&T gateway.
24.2 UDP 500
IKEv2 typically begins negotiation using UDP port 500.
The peers exchange cryptographic proposals, nonces, Diffie-Hellman information, and NAT detection information.
24.3 NAT detection
IKEv2 includes NAT detection payloads that allow peers to determine whether the IP addresses or ports have been translated along the network path.
When NAT is detected, the connection can transition to NAT Traversal.
24.4 UDP 4500
NAT Traversal encapsulates IPsec ESP traffic inside UDP.
This allows NAT devices to maintain translation entries using UDP ports.
Protocol
Purpose
UDP 500
Initial IKE negotiation
UDP 4500
IKE and UDP-encapsulated ESP with NAT-T
ESP protocol 50
Native IPsec ESP when UDP encapsulation is not used

24.5 Why the remote port was different
The recovered crypto session output showed that the physical router was using UDP 4500 locally while the remote peer appeared with a translated UDP source port.
Sanitized representation:
IKEv2 SA:
 local <R2_UNDERLAY_IP>/4500
 remote <TRANSLATED_REMOTE_IP>/<TRANSLATED_UDP_PORT>
 Active
This behavior is consistent with NAT Traversal.
The remote peer does not necessarily appear with source port 4500 after passing through NAT.
A translated source port is not automatically evidence of a VPN problem.
24.6 Home gateway requirements
The existing residential gateway already supported inbound VPN connectivity for the Windows remote-access VPN.
The relevant inbound services were:
UDP 500  -> R2
UDP 4500 -> R2
The dedicated CML tunnel reused the same physical VPN headend.
The IKEv2 profile and authentication configuration distinguished the CML connection from the Windows remote-access connection.
Separate external UDP ports were not required simply because two different VPN profiles existed.
25. Phase 18 — IKEv2 negotiation process
The following sequence explains what occurred logically when V1 initiated the tunnel.
25.1 IKE_SA_INIT
During the initial exchange, the peers negotiate:
Encryption algorithm.
Integrity algorithm.
Pseudorandom function.
Diffie-Hellman group.
Nonces.
NAT detection.
25.2 IKE_AUTH
During authentication, the peers exchange and validate identities and authentication information.
The pre-shared key is used to calculate authentication data. The key itself is not simply transmitted as plaintext to the other router.
The responder selects the matching IKEv2 profile.
25.3 Authorization
The configured authorization policy determines the routing and interface information exchanged between the peers.
25.4 CHILD SA
The initial IPsec CHILD SA is negotiated during IKE_AUTH.
The CHILD SA protects actual data traffic using the selected IPsec transform set.
25.5 Dynamic tunnel instantiation
R2 creates a Virtual-Access interface derived from Virtual-Template2.
Once the required security associations and routes are established, the routers can exchange protected traffic.
