30. Problem 5 — Restricting IKEv2 route acceptance
30.1 Security concern
The final authorization policy included:
route accept any
This allows the authenticated peer to supply routes without a narrowly defined route filter at that configuration point.
While acceptable for controlled lab testing, this introduces a potential routing security concern.
An incorrectly configured or compromised authenticated peer could potentially supply unintended routing information.
30.2 Investigating IOS capabilities
The following exact command interaction was recovered:
R2#configure terminal

R2(config)#crypto ikev2 authorization policy CML-AUTH

R2(config-ikev2-author-policy)#route ?
  accept  Route accept parameters
  set     Specify route set parameters
Further inspection:
R2(config-ikev2-author-policy)#route accept ?
  any  Accept all subnets from peer
The IOS version only exposed any under the inspected route accept command.
No direct prefix-list or route-map option appeared in that command context.
30.3 Final decision
Restricting IKEv2 route acceptance was deferred rather than making unsupported changes to the working configuration.
The authorization policy remained:
crypto ikev2 authorization policy CML-AUTH
 route set interface
 route accept any
This was a documented limitation of the current implementation.
It does not mean that every possible route-filtering mechanism on the platform was investigated or that no alternative security control exists.
Future hardening should evaluate platform-supported authorization controls and routing policy mechanisms.
