36. Complete packet walk through the final tunnel
The following describes a packet generated by V1 and destined for the physical R2 overlay address.
Step 1 — Packet generation
V1 generates an IP packet using its tunnel interface address as the source.
Inner source:      <V1_CML_TUNNEL_IP>
Inner destination: <R2_CML_TUNNEL_IP>
Step 2 — Routing lookup
V1 performs a routing lookup.
The destination is reachable through Tunnel100.
Step 3 — IPsec processing
Tunnel100 references the CML-IPSEC profile.
The router identifies the appropriate outbound IPsec security association.
Step 4 — Encryption
The original packet is encrypted using the negotiated ESP security association.
The original inner addressing remains protected inside the encrypted payload.
Step 5 — Outer encapsulation
V1 creates the outer packet using its underlay interface and the physical VPN endpoint.
Outer source:      <V1_UNDERLAY_IP>
Outer destination: <HOME_PUBLIC_IP>
Step 6 — NAT Traversal
Because NAT exists along the path, ESP is encapsulated inside UDP.
Step 7 — VMware translation
VMware NAT translates the outer source address and potentially the UDP source port.
The encrypted inner packet is not rewritten by VMware NAT.
Step 8 — Internet transport
The packet travels through the remote access network and Internet toward the residential gateway.
Step 9 — Residential gateway forwarding
The AT&T gateway forwards the relevant VPN traffic toward R2.
Step 10 — IPsec decapsulation
R2 identifies the incoming IPsec security association.
The router validates the integrity of the packet and decrypts the encrypted payload.
Step 11 — Logical tunnel processing
The recovered inner packet is associated with the active Virtual-Access interface.
Step 12 — Destination processing
R2 recognizes the destination as its own overlay address and processes the packet.
Step 13 — Return routing
R2 generates a reply.
The learned route toward V1's tunnel address identifies the active Virtual-Access interface.
Step 14 — Return encryption
R2 encrypts the reply and sends it toward the translated remote peer.
Step 15 — Reverse NAT processing
The upstream NAT devices use their existing translation state to deliver the encrypted reply to V1.
Step 16 — Final decapsulation
V1 decrypts the reply and delivers the original packet to the appropriate local process.
This establishes bidirectional communication across the encrypted overlay without requiring Windows to forward the original inner IP packets.
