39. Long-term stability monitoring
An active tunnel at a single point in time does not establish long-term reliability.
The connection should be monitored across:
IKEv2 rekeying.
IPsec CHILD SA rekeying.
VMware virtual machine restarts.
V1 restarts.
R2 restarts.
Windows host sleep or restart.
Wireless network reconnects.
NAT translation changes.
Public IP address changes.
Tunnel disconnections and automatic recovery.
Extended packet transmission.
Simultaneous Windows remote-access VPN usage.
39.1 Monitoring commands
show crypto session

show crypto ikev2 sa detailed

show crypto ipsec sa

show logging

show processes cpu sorted

show memory statistics

show ip route <REMOTE_TUNNEL_IP>
39.2 Extended connectivity testing
ping <REMOTE_TUNNEL_IP> repeat 100

ping <REMOTE_TUNNEL_IP> repeat 1000
These tests can help identify packet loss and intermittent connectivity.
39.3 MTU considerations
IPsec and NAT Traversal introduce additional packet overhead.
This reduces the maximum amount of inner packet data that can be transported without fragmentation.
If larger packets fail while small packets succeed, the tunnel MTU and TCP MSS should be investigated.
Possible future tuning commands:
interface Tunnel100
 ip mtu 1400
 ip tcp adjust-mss 1360
These are illustrative values, not measured settings from the completed implementation.
The correct MTU should be determined through testing rather than assumed.
