44. Technical accomplishments
This implementation involved substantially more than configuring a basic IPsec tunnel.
It required integrating virtualization, physical networking, routing, authentication, encryption, and NAT across independent network environments.
The completed work demonstrated practical experience with:
Network engineering
Subnet analysis, overlap resolution, static routing, return paths and interface addressing.
Network security
IKEv2, IPsec, PSK authentication, cryptographic proposals and traffic selectors.
Virtualization
CML, VMware VMnet8, System Bridge and virtual network connectivity.
Cisco FlexVPN
Static VTI, dynamic VTI, Virtual-Template and Virtual-Access interfaces.
NAT Traversal
UDP encapsulation, translated source ports and multiple NAT boundaries.
Troubleshooting
Cisco IOS CLI, Windows PowerShell, route inspection and crypto session analysis.
Architecture design
Separation of remote-access and router-to-router VPN infrastructure.
Routing preparation
Establishment of a routed encrypted transport for future BGP peering.
The most significant architectural improvement was replacing the attempted Windows-based transit routing design with an independent Cisco router-to-router FlexVPN connection.
The resulting architecture provides a dedicated encrypted Layer 3 transport between CML and the physical Cisco lab, while retaining the existing certificate-based Windows remote-access VPN as a separate service.
Final confirmed state: the dedicated CML IKEv2/IPsec session reached UP-ACTIVE, the dynamic Virtual-Access interface was instantiated, and R2 learned the remote tunnel address. The architecture is ready for the next stage of BGP configuration, subject to completing the remaining persistence, connectivity, security-hardening, and stability checks.
